WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.660
Listening comprehension, as I've
said before, is the core skill

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.540
that leads us to speaking better.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.010
Leads us to reading better.

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:11.280
Leads us to better, a better
sense of the language.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:15.510
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here and today,
I wanna talk about listening the most

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.730
powerful activity and language learning.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.640
Remember if you enjoy my videos,
please subscribe, click on

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.020
the bell for notifications.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.800
If you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:32.330
So I've spoken a lot about listening, but
today I was reminded of how tremendously

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:34.610
powerful a learning activity it is.

00:00:35.269 --> 00:00:39.270
I had to spend much of the day
cleaning up around the house.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.830
We're donating some books and
other material to the thrift store

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.600
getting rid of a lot of garbage.

00:00:45.605 --> 00:00:47.880
So I had to go to the recycling depot.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:51.210
So there was a good an hour and
a half, two hours between working

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:57.720
and driving and I listened to my
Persian podcast Bplus podcast.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:04.230
Now, first of all, what's powerful is
that I'm able to use all this time,

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:05.910
time that would otherwise be dead.

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:14.250
Second of all, uh, this Bplus podcast, uh,
this Ali Bandari, I think it is, he talks

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:15.660
about a variety of different subjects.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.914
What's of interest to me
is the history of Iran.

00:01:19.185 --> 00:01:22.185
So he talks about the Safavid Dynasty.

00:01:22.245 --> 00:01:28.074
He talks about different Kings,
Shah of the Safavid Dynasty, Shah

00:01:28.414 --> 00:01:30.825
Ismail, Shah Abbas, and so forth.

00:01:31.035 --> 00:01:34.905
And of course, I have heard some of
this stuff before, but I get it again

00:01:34.905 --> 00:01:36.585
in a slightly different context.

00:01:36.765 --> 00:01:40.815
So I'm learning, not deliberately
learning, not taking notes, but

00:01:40.815 --> 00:01:45.184
gradualy things are becoming
clearer to me through listening.

00:01:45.725 --> 00:01:49.354
And I'm, I'm reminded of the fact
that throughout history for most of

00:01:49.354 --> 00:01:51.365
recorded history, we had no writing.

00:01:51.365 --> 00:01:53.585
People couldn't read
like, yes, the first...

00:01:53.824 --> 00:01:58.655
I know one of the earliest, in fact,
examples of writing is, is this

00:01:58.655 --> 00:02:00.615
writing on these clay cylinders?

00:02:00.634 --> 00:02:08.400
And in fact, uh, Darius's famous, kind of
the, one of the first written documents

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:14.550
about human rights was, was written in
cuneiform script on these clay cylinders.

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:19.050
And, but if we go through history, you
know, the, again, going back to that

00:02:19.055 --> 00:02:25.800
time, you know, the legend of Gilgamesh,
or, uh, the Odyssey or, or the, uh,

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:32.955
the Iliad or, uh, in, in south and
Southeast Asia, the Ramayana stories,

00:02:32.955 --> 00:02:34.695
stories from China, from elsewhere.

00:02:34.815 --> 00:02:37.455
People have had stories
and people listened.

00:02:37.785 --> 00:02:42.705
It's just that they didn't have MP3
players and they listened more than once.

00:02:43.185 --> 00:02:45.705
And we all have this as children.

00:02:45.705 --> 00:02:48.915
We had our favorite stories and these...

00:02:49.215 --> 00:02:53.895
in order for the, the Iliad the Ramayana
or any of these famous stories to be

00:02:53.895 --> 00:02:59.070
passed on from generation to generation
people told these stories more than once.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:02.130
And they were favorite stories
and people were happy to listen

00:03:02.135 --> 00:03:03.390
to them over and over again.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.050
And they enjoyed different aspects of
the story every time they listened.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.950
And, and similarly, you know, as
I'm listening, as I said, in one

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:14.070
of my previous videos, I don't
deliberately try to understand.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.560
And of course I lose
focus from time to time.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:20.520
And I'm sure that, uh, children sitting
around at campfire, listening to a

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:24.150
story every so often they, you know,
look somewhere else and they lose

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:25.540
focus and then they come back to it.

00:03:25.545 --> 00:03:30.985
It doesn't matter, but it's such a
powerful means of bringing knowledge

00:03:31.045 --> 00:03:37.350
into your brain and it's it, it,
you know, evokes the the subject

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.420
in a way that video can't do.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:45.570
Uh, I remember when I, uh, I got these,
uh, storytellers, Chinese storytellers

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:51.540
telling The Romance of the Three
Kingdoms and, uh, the man's voice and

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:55.320
the way he conjured up horses and dust,
I could almost smell the dust and see

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:57.390
the action, because it was all words.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:04.230
And when I got to see a video of the same
story, It, it was a pale, uh, version

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:10.260
of the same, far less, uh, left to the
imagination, obviously fewer words.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.870
So, so this listening is so powerful.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.860
Uh, you'll see, on my, uh, shot of
my screen at LingQ that I've been,

00:04:17.609 --> 00:04:21.029
you know, listening to these stories
and studying the text so that I

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:23.280
understand them a bit better each time.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:25.590
Uh, but I've never looked...

00:04:25.739 --> 00:04:26.609
and these are videos.

00:04:26.609 --> 00:04:31.590
These are YouTube videos, and I'm sure
he shows a lot of stuff and pictures of

00:04:31.594 --> 00:04:34.080
these, uh, Persian Kings and whatever.

00:04:34.085 --> 00:04:37.500
But, uh, I just listen,
I just listen and read.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.309
And so I have an image
incomplete, somewhat imperfect.

00:04:41.309 --> 00:04:42.030
It doesn't matter.

00:04:42.059 --> 00:04:43.789
My knowledge is accumulating.

00:04:44.085 --> 00:04:46.515
Every time I listen, I, I
gather a few more words.

00:04:46.575 --> 00:04:51.525
I get a different sense of these Kings
and their time and Isfahan and different

00:04:51.525 --> 00:04:56.445
aspects of the history, the culture,
the geography of Iran and some words.

00:04:56.445 --> 00:04:59.385
And it's all gradually
accumulating without any deliberate

00:04:59.415 --> 00:05:01.425
effort on my, on my part.

00:05:01.725 --> 00:05:03.255
But it's, it's, it's accumulating.

00:05:03.255 --> 00:05:07.380
It's accumulating like, like ,you
know, uh, I've often used the image

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.610
in, in language running of this...

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:12.540
if you start rolling a snowball down a
hill, it'll gradually pick up more and

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.610
more snow and, and that's how it is.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.080
And yet it's so portable.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.130
It's so easy to do.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:20.790
If I had to watch his videos, I would
have to sit down and watch them.

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:25.140
Uh, and I'm gonna do a video about
reading, which is powerful in its own

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:30.090
way, because it's a lovely environment
to get into just, you know, you and the

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:34.099
text, uh, without the sound, without
the audio, just you and the text.

00:05:34.489 --> 00:05:37.485
That's also powerful, but reading
is something that has come

00:05:37.485 --> 00:05:38.985
relatively lately to humans.

00:05:39.075 --> 00:05:44.235
And, and in particular, the vast majority
of humans didn't read until about

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:46.965
the 15th, 16th, 17th, 18th century.

00:05:47.355 --> 00:05:49.605
And so they relied on the spoken word.

00:05:49.845 --> 00:05:53.835
They relied on familiar stories,
told over and over again, uh, as

00:05:53.835 --> 00:05:57.015
a means of acquiring information
as a means of entertainment.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:03.390
And we now can use that same, uh,
the wonder of storytelling as a

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:08.130
means of acquiring knowledge as a
means of acquiring, uh, language

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:09.780
as a form of entertainment.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:15.090
And, uh, and of course the means of
delivering this have just proliferated.

00:06:15.095 --> 00:06:20.310
So we have podcasts and we have,
uh, you know, audio books and

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:22.590
different services, podcast services.

00:06:23.355 --> 00:06:24.525
Audiobook services.

00:06:24.855 --> 00:06:29.085
And so this, this rich world where,
you know, it used to be that we'd

00:06:29.085 --> 00:06:35.715
have to, uh, maybe sit in a, in a
room somewhere or sit outside, um,

00:06:36.045 --> 00:06:41.655
underneath the trees to listen to
the storyteller spin his or her tale.

00:06:41.955 --> 00:06:45.565
Very often a story that we were
familiar with in the olden days

00:06:46.045 --> 00:06:48.120
and now we just carry that with us.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:52.260
I mean, how many stories can I keep
in how many different languages, just

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:55.680
in my little iPhone and I can listen
to whichever one I wanna listen to.

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:58.530
I can even shuffle them and listen
to them in different orders.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:00.270
I can go to different languages.

00:07:00.719 --> 00:07:03.180
Uh, all of those things
are now available to us.

00:07:03.719 --> 00:07:10.020
Uh, and I think so listening is not
only a very effective way of learning,

00:07:10.409 --> 00:07:15.420
listening comprehension, as I've said
before, is the core skill that leads

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:20.490
us to speaking better, leads us to
reading better, leads us to better, a

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:24.240
better sense of the language, but it
is also a major form of entertainment.

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:27.240
It's a major form of enjoying the
language, particularly when we don't

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:30.150
have the opportunity to be surrounded
by people speaking the language.

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:35.280
There are so many means today
through technology whereby we can

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:39.750
access the language and enjoyment
and knowledge through listening.

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:44.010
And so, and, and another thing about
listening too, is it's kind of easy to do.

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:48.120
So it gets you going, uh, as
I've said before, very often the

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:49.770
main thing is to get started.

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:55.350
So even in terms of a day, you know, today
I had to do some chores around the house.

00:07:55.664 --> 00:07:58.905
But I start listening now because
I've been listening and not

00:07:58.905 --> 00:08:00.734
understanding a fair amount of it.

00:08:00.945 --> 00:08:03.365
I am now my, my curiosity is piqued.

00:08:03.645 --> 00:08:07.784
It triggers my reading and it
just keeps me going down the

00:08:07.789 --> 00:08:09.044
path of learning the language.

00:08:09.224 --> 00:08:13.065
So I just wanted, again, I wanted
to touch on this, the importance of

00:08:13.065 --> 00:08:17.685
listening as the easiest thing you
can do in order to create the time and

00:08:17.924 --> 00:08:20.044
push yourself forward in your language.

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:23.340
And I'll leave you a couple
of videos about listening

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:24.300
that I've done in the past.

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:27.150
And hopefully again, I
don't contradict myself.

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:28.410
Thank you for listening.

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:29.250
Bye for now.

